In chemistry, the sedimentation coefficient (s) of a particle characterizes its sedimentation (tendency to settle out of suspension) during centrifugation. It is defined as the ratio of a particle's sedimentation velocity to the applied acceleration causing the sedimentation. s = v t a {\displaystyle s={\frac {v_{t}}{a}}}

The sedimentation speed v is also the terminal velocity. It is constant because the force applied to a particle by gravity or by a centrifuge (typically in multiples of tens of thousands of gravities in an ultracentrifuge) is balanced by the viscous resistance (or "drag") of the fluid (normally water) through which the particle is moving. The applied acceleration a can be either the gravitational acceleration g, or more commonly the centrifugal acceleration ω 2 r. In the latter case, ω is the angular velocity of the rotor and r is the distance of a particle to the rotor axis (radius).

